# Dynamis · Design System

## Color psychology (decided May 20, 2026)

The palette is **deliberately a timeline**: blue = past/memory, red = present/achievement, green = future/suggestion. Use these meanings consistently.

| Color | Hex | Semantic role | Where it appears |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Blue (memory)** | `#1B5E9F` primary, `#2E86DE` accent, `#DCE9F7` soft | Brand, context, memory, information, profile, history | Primary buttons (when contextual), brand mark, info cards, profile sections |
| **Red (achievement)** | `#D94A38` primary, `#C73E2C` deep, `#E8654D` warm | Energeia, progress, streaks, achievements, realized goals | Streak indicators, Possibility Map "Energeia" side glow, high-priority tags, achievement leaves on the tree, the Awakening CTA |
| **Green (suggestion)** | `#2EBC8F` primary, `#15803D` deep | Suggestions, next steps, completed checkmarks, growth, tree foliage | Council suggestion pills, plan item checkboxes (when done), the tree's leaves, "next step" CTAs, Daily Reflection icon |

### Color rules — non-negotiable

1. **NEVER use gold, amber, or yellow as a primary palette color.** This was reverted from an earlier mockup. Specifically forbidden hex values: `#F4B942`, `#FFC857`, `#E0A92C`, `#FFE9A8`. If a generated component uses these, replace with the red palette equivalents.
2. **Red is for achievement and momentum, NOT for errors or destructive actions.** For destructive UI use a distinct cooler tone (`#9B2C2C`) — preferably handle destructive actions with confirmation modals instead of color alone.
3. **Color is never the only signal.** Always pair with an icon, shape, or label — for accessibility (red/green together is the classic colorblind-problematic pair, and we use both).

### Background and surface

- Default theme is **light**, not dark. `--bg: #FAFBFD`, surface white.
- **Two screens use dark backgrounds**: The Awakening (cinematic radial gradient, `#0B1929` → `#050D17`) and the Possibility Map (same dark gradient — the contrast makes the Energeia constellation glow).
- All other screens are light. Dark mode is out of scope for the POC.

## Typography

- **Display / headlines**: `Fraunces` (Google Fonts) — variable, weight 500-700, opsz set to use larger optical sizes. Italic Fraunces is used for emphasis inside headlines (`<em>` tag).
- **Body / UI**: `Manrope` (Google Fonts) — weights 400, 500, 600, 700.
- Forbidden defaults: Inter, Roboto, Arial, system-ui as the primary font.
- Tabular numbers for stats: `font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums` on streaks, counts, percentages.

## Spacing and rhythm

- Base unit: 4px. Use multiples (4, 8, 12, 16, 20, 24, 32, 48, 64, 80).
- Card border radius: `16px` (small), `20-22px` (medium), `44px` (device frame).
- Pills/chips: `100px` border radius (fully rounded).

## i18n in components (per `04-i18n-english-first`)

The mockups show **conceptual copy** like "AI is transforming your world". In real code, all user-facing strings go through `t()`:

```tsx
// ❌ Don't — hardcoded string
<h1>AI is transforming your world.</h1>

// ✅ Do — translation key with product vocabulary
<h1>{t('awakening.headline.line1')}</h1>
```

Define keys using product terminology (see `11-dynamis-terminology`):

```
awakening.headline.line1 = "AI is transforming your world."
awakening.headline.line2 = "Are you?"
possibilityMap.dynamis.label = "Potential, waiting to be realized."
possibilityMap.energeia.label = "Potential, becoming actuality."
```

## Visual references

The two mockups are the source of truth:
- `Dynamis_POC_Mockup_v2.html` — desktop layouts, sidebar nav, dark/light contrast
- `Dynamis_Mobile_v2.html` — mobile layouts, bottom tab bar, iOS device frame

When generating a new component, look at the closest analog in these files first.

## What to avoid

- Purple gradients on white (generic AI aesthetic).
- Emoji as decoration. Single emoji (🔥 for streak) used meaningfully is fine.
- Solid color backgrounds for hero sections — use radial gradients with sparks (see Awakening).
- Generic Tailwind defaults (`bg-blue-500`, `text-gray-700`). Map to our token palette.

## CSS variable naming

Use the existing variables in the mockups as the canonical set:

```css
--bg: #FAFBFD;
--bg-soft: #F1F5FA;
--bg-deep: #0B1929;
--ink: #0B1929;        /* primary text */
--ink-2: #475569;      /* secondary text */
--ink-3: #94A3B8;      /* tertiary, hints */
--line: #E2E8F0;
--line-soft: #EEF2F7;
--blue: #1B5E9F;
--blue-2: #2E86DE;
--blue-soft: #DCE9F7;
--red: #D94A38;        /* achievement primary */
--red-2: #C73E2C;      /* achievement deep */
--red-warm: #E8654D;   /* achievement light */
--success: #2EBC8F;    /* suggestion / completed */
```

If the project uses Tailwind, extend `theme.colors` to map these tokens. Do not introduce new color values without updating this file first.
